    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking at some data regarding thermal management in high-performance braking systems. Specifically, I'm comparing the heat dissipation rates of carbon-ceramic discs versus semi-metallic pads during repeated high-speed deceleration cycles. Can you explain the primary mechanisms of heat soak in these materials and how they contribute to brake fade during heavy use?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Heat soak occurs when frictional energy cannot dissipate quickly enough through conduction and convection, causing the disc or pad temperature to rise into the material's immutable phase-change or softening zones. In carbon-ceramic discs, excessive heat can soften the resin binder, reducing's coefficient of friction, while semi-metallic pads suffer as fumes from glazing and uneven pad wear degrade surface performance. This thermal runaway creates brake fade, where repeated high-speed deceleration cycles result in a significant loss of braking power due to these thermally induced material changes.",
      "source": "target",
